# Meend Acid

**Ingredients:** SARANGI + ACID HOUSE / TB-303 LANGUAGE + PREPARED ELECTRIC PIANO + KHANJIRA / WOODBLOCK + BASS CLARINET + SHORT ANALOG KICK

**Creative Catalyst:** The genre began during a monsoon-season residency inside a decommissioned rainfall-calibration station outside Pune, where sarangi player Ananya Rao was practicing slow meend glides against a constant tanpura reference while electronic producer Leon Mercer tested stripped acid-house patterns through a hardware sequencer. The breakthrough came when Leon stopped writing 303 lines as independent bass hooks and instead treated the sarangi’s continuous pitch movement as the source of harmonic tension. Rather than making the synth imitate the bowed glide, he froze the acid line into short repeated pitch cells and automated only filter cutoff, resonance, accent, and decay while Ananya moved freely between notes above it. Prepared electric piano, khanjira, woodblock, and bass clarinet completed a form where one instrument bends pitch while the other bends timbre.

Meend Acid is a sparse acoustic-electronic dance style built around a deliberate separation between pitch motion and timbral motion. Acid house supplies the chassis: 118–126 BPM, dry four-on-the-floor or lightly broken kick, clipped clap, economical hats, short 303-like sequencer cells, resonance sweeps, accents, slides used sparingly, and long stretches where the note pattern barely changes. The sarangi supplies the opposing logic through bowed sustain, meend, andolan-like oscillation, microtonal approach tones, sympathetic-string shimmer, pressure changes, and vocal-like phrasing that can pass continuously through the spaces between fixed pitches.

The sarangi is recorded with one close condenser near the bowing area, one ribbon microphone toward the body and sympathetic-string field, and one restrained room microphone. The close channel captures bow grain, left-hand pressure, attack noise, and the onset of glides. The ribbon provides wood, low-mid body, and sympathetic resonance. The room rises only during long held transitions so the acoustic tail can widen without becoming a cinematic wash.

Players favor tonic-centered modal phrases, slow glides between minor third and fourth, second-to-tonic falls, fifth-to-sixth suspensions, narrow oscillations around important notes, open fifth implication, and long held tones that expose bow pressure. Fast virtuosic taans are minimized. The defining gesture is a continuous pitch path over an electronic pattern that refuses to follow it.

The central compositional device is **Pitch-Still / Timbre-Moving**. The acid sequencer repeats a very small pitch cell—often one, two, or three notes—while filter cutoff, resonance, accent, envelope depth, and decay change over time. Above it, the sarangi travels through continuous pitch space. The listener therefore hears two forms of movement: the acoustic instrument changes note without discrete steps, while the electronic instrument changes color without changing melodic identity.

A second device, **Meend Window**, protects selected glides by simplifying the rhythm and freezing the acid parameters for a short interval. During the beginning of the glide, hats may disappear and the 303 filter remains static. Once the sarangi reaches its destination pitch, the acid resonance begins moving again. This makes arrival rather than departure the trigger for electronic motion.

A third device, **Resonance Bias**, assigns different acid-filter behaviors to different sarangi destinations without directly tracking pitch. A glide resolving to the tonic may produce a darker, shorter filter envelope; arrival on the fourth may open resonance and decay; arrival on the sixth may trigger a brighter accent pattern. These relationships are arranged manually and remain musical rather than sensor-driven.

Drums are dry and disciplined. Kick is compact and regular enough to sustain dance momentum without overwhelming the bow texture. Clap or snare is short. Closed hats use restrained eighths or sixteenths with deliberate gaps. Open hats are rare. Percussion fills are short and usually acoustic rather than programmed.

The acid voice uses one mono synth line in the TB-303 tradition but avoids constant squelch. Pitch cells remain intentionally simple. Slides are used only when they create contrast with the sarangi rather than copying it. Resonance peaks are automated gradually, and high resonance is reserved for structural sections. The bass line should feel stubborn and geometrically fixed while the sarangi remains fluid.

Prepared electric piano provides dry harmonic checkpoints. Selected tines are muted with removable felt or paper. Voicings use open fifths, suspended seconds, minor sevenths, modal thirds, and widely spaced octaves. The piano rarely sustains under a long sarangi glide. Instead, it confirms important arrival pitches after the glide resolves.

Khanjira and woodblock provide acoustic punctuation. Khanjira uses muted bass strokes, fingertip snaps, pitch-bend pressure, and short rolls. Woodblock provides dry narrow transients at phrase boundaries. These parts replace selected hats, claps, or percussion hits rather than continuously doubling the electronic rhythm.

Bass clarinet occupies the low-mid acoustic register with breath-heavy fundamentals, short falling seconds, chalumeau pedals, key noise, and restrained split tones. It enters mainly when the acid line thins or disappears, preserving low-register weight without introducing another synth layer.

Vocals are optional and sparse. Marathi, Hindi, or English fragments may reference rainfall gauges, pressure bias, station logs, wet calibration marks, arrival points, or instructions such as “hold the line,” “bend above,” “do not follow,” and “arrive first.” Delivery is spoken, close, and understated.

Effects remain restrained. Sarangi stays mostly dry with a short room and occasional compact plate. Acid synth gets light saturation and short spring or plate reflection only at major transitions. Electric piano uses a short plate. Bass clarinet gets a dark chamber. Drum bus compression is firm but not pumping. Long trance delays, supersaw pads, cinematic Indian strings, psychedelic sitar clichés, and synth lines that mirror the sarangi note-for-note are excluded.

The defining tension is continuous pitch against discrete sequence. The sarangi bends where the acid line stays still; the acid line mutates where the sarangi finally arrives.

## Genre DNA

**Tempo & Pulse:** 118–126 BPM, mostly 4/4 acid-house pulse with compact kick, clipped clap, restrained hats, short mono sequencer cells, and sarangi phrases that float across barlines through slow meend.

**Harmonic Palette:** Dorian, Aeolian, Kafi-adjacent modal color used non-dogmatically, tonic-fifth anchors, suspended seconds, modal thirds, minor sevenths, sixth suspensions, and long pedal-tone zones.

**Production Signatures:**

1. **Pitch-Still / Timbre-Moving** — the acid pitch cell remains nearly fixed while filter and envelope parameters evolve under continuous sarangi pitch motion.
2. **Meend Window** — drums and acid modulation simplify during exposed glides; electronic movement resumes after the sarangi arrives.
3. **Resonance Bias** — different sarangi arrival tones correspond to manually arranged acid-filter behaviors without direct pitch tracking.
4. **Arrival Piano** — prepared electric piano confirms destination pitches after major glides rather than during them.
5. **Acoustic Percussion Substitution** — khanjira and woodblock replace selected machine events instead of becoming a parallel percussion layer.
6. **No-Melodic-Chase Rule** — synth and bass clarinet never track the sarangi glide note-for-note.

**Dynamics:** Intensity grows through stronger bow pressure, wider meend spans, brighter acid resonance, denser accents, harder khanjira attacks, lower piano punctures, and shorter gaps between arrival events. Climaxes remain taut and danceable rather than maximal.

## Scene & Origin Lore

Meend Acid formed inside a retired rainfall-calibration and hydrology station on the outskirts of Pune. The site retained graduated rain tanks, pressure gauges, metal collection funnels, wet concrete channels, chart recorders, blue filing cabinets, handwritten monsoon tables, stainless workbenches, and long windows streaked by seasonal rain.

Sarangi player **Ananya Rao** had been using the station to rehearse because the dry control room made sympathetic-string resonance easy to judge even during heavy rain outside. She became interested in the difference between a note as a destination and the continuous path leading toward it.

Electronic producer **Leon Mercer** was working nearby with a small analog sequencer and acid-style mono synth. His first collaboration used a busy 303 line that copied fragments of Ananya’s melody. The result sounded decorative and redundant. He deleted most of the pitches, reduced the sequence to two notes, and let the filter become the moving part instead. The contrast was immediate: Ananya’s bow carried pitch through continuous space while the synth seemed locked in place but constantly changing surface.

Prepared-electric-piano player **Meera Kulkarni** joined after developing short arrival chords that only appeared after glides had resolved. Percussionist **Sameer Naik** reduced his setup to khanjira and woodblock so acoustic accents could replace machine events without crowding the groove. Bass-clarinetist **Jon Bell** completed the ensemble with dark low air for acid-free sections.

Their first performances were called **bias tests**. A small board near the stage displayed three words—START, BEND, ARRIVE—and a separate column labeled FILTER. Nothing was automated from the board. It existed only to teach the audience that pitch motion and timbral motion were intentionally assigned to different instruments.

The visual language developed around rain-gauge silver, monsoon blue, wet concrete grey, chart-paper cream, indicator red, raw sarangi wood, matte black electronics, and fluorescent green. The unofficial rule is that if the acid line starts singing the same melody as the sarangi, the arrangement has failed.

