# Fictional EP Tracklist

## 1. Gauge Five Refuses to Follow the Bend

**Story & Context:** At 124 BPM, Ananya opens with a slow sarangi glide from the second toward the tonic while Leon repeats a two-note acid cell without pitch change. During the glide, the filter remains nearly closed and hats disappear. The instant Ananya settles on the tonic, Leon opens resonance and shortens decay for four bars. Meera answers with one prepared-piano fifth after the arrival. Sameer replaces the expected clap with a dry khanjira snap every second cycle. Jon remains silent until the acid line drops out, then enters with one low chalumeau fundamental. The central section repeats the same electronic notes under a wider sarangi glide toward the fourth, proving that the arrangement can feel harmonically transformed while the sequencer pitch content barely changes.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Rain gauge marked 5 with one bowed curve rising across a fixed two-step electronic grid, water droplets on chart paper, sarangi bridge and analog filter knob in shallow focus. Sparse fragments limited to “gauge five,” “do not follow,” “bend above,” and “arrive first.”

## 2. Monsoon Table Shows the Same Two Notes

**Story & Context:** At 121 BPM, Leon uses the EP’s most repetitive acid cell: only two pitches for almost the entire track. Variation comes from accent placement, cutoff, envelope depth, and decay. Ananya moves through three different modal routes above the same sequence, using narrow meend, wider fifth-to-sixth suspension, and a long descending phrase back to tonic. Every major glide creates a Meend Window where the drum grid thins. Meera plays one dry minor-seventh dyad after each arrival. Sameer alternates khanjira bass strokes with woodblock hits. Jon contributes short falling bass-clarinet seconds during the lowest-resonance sections. The track’s hook is hearing the same electronic notes feel different because the bowed context keeps changing.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Handwritten monsoon table with the same two pitch names repeated down an entire column while curved ink lines bend across them, rain pooling around metal calibration rulers. Sparse fragments limited to “same two notes,” “table unchanged,” “water moved,” and “filter opens.”

## 3. Arrival Pressure Recorded at 3:12 A.M.

**Story & Context:** The closer runs at 118 BPM and begins without the acid line. Ananya sustains one low sarangi tone with slow pressure oscillation while Meera places isolated prepared-piano octaves and Sameer uses fingertip khanjira taps. Leon introduces a single-note acid pulse after sixteen bars, then gradually raises resonance without changing pitch. Jon adds breath-heavy bass-clarinet pedals beneath selected bow releases. In the final minute, Ananya performs the widest glide on the EP, taking several bars to travel from a low tonic toward the fifth. Everything enters a long Meend Window: hats vanish, acid parameters freeze, piano stops. Only after the sarangi fully settles does the synth filter open to its brightest point. The track ends on that contrast—stable pitch in both instruments, maximum timbral difference between them.

